From 22dc585531964478c78f5853dab92c4869aca72b Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Nicole Muzzin <nicks@fuckin.email>
Date: Sun, 4 Oct 2026 05:41:43 -0400
Subject: [PATCH] script: HTMLImageElement.loading and lazy loading of images
 and iframes

img.loading was undefined and loading=lazy was ignored, so feeds that
rely on it fetched everything up front, and the HTML parser's
prefetcher fetched lazy images too.

The loading attribute now reflects as an enumerated attribute (missing
and invalid default to eager per spec; Chrome returns "auto" there).
Update the image data defers the fetch of a lazy image: it stops
delaying the load event, records the lazy load resumption steps and
starts intersection-observing the element through the document's lazy
load intersection observer, an internal IntersectionObserver whose
callback runs the resumption steps of intersecting targets. Switching
the attribute to eager resumes immediately. iframes get the same for
src navigations (srcdoc already recorded its resumption steps but never
observed the element). The prefetcher skips loading=lazy images like
Chrome's preload scanner.

The observer grows both its root (the viewport) and scroll containers
by 1250px, Chrome's distance on a fast connection: the spec's scroll
margin alone never reaches the viewport. A document in a cross-origin
iframe cannot reach the top-level document from its script thread, so
its observer uses its own viewport rather than never firing.

IntersectionObserver also never cleared its connected flag when its
last target was unobserved, so a later observe() left it out of the
document's observer list for good. Any page that unobserves and then
observes again (and the lazy load observer after its first load) lost
all notifications.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
